WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968
-He is a talented author

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.803
and Booker Prize finalist
whose new book,

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:06.072
"Filthy Animals,"
was published today.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.007
Please welcome to the show
Brandon Taylor.

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:10.409
How are you, Brandon?
-Oh, great.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:13.379
Thanks for having me.
-I am thrilled to have you.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.783
Now, your first novel
is being adapted into a film.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.417
You're writing the screenplay.

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:21.020
And now your second book
is out today,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.388
and you're here to promote it.

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:26.826
Where have you ended up
insofar as what you predicted

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:29.729
going into your writing career?

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:33.600
-Well, the very outer reaches
of the solar system,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.368
I would say.
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.237
With my first book,
I thought, "Okay.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:38.905
Five people will read this,

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:41.841
four of which
are my family members maybe

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:44.877
and maybe that one friend
who shows up to everything

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:47.680
despite there not being
a thing to show up to."

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.516
But, I mean, I've just been
blown away by the way

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.951
that readers have, you know,
taken me in

00:00:52.951 --> 00:00:55.054
and the way that they've loved
this book, and, yeah.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:01:00.660
So all of my expectations met,
exceeded even.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.496
-So, you -- before you got your
MFA -- I'm fascinated by this --

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.698
you were a student biochemist.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:09.569
And I'm wondering
if you completely left

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.104
that learning behind
or if you brought along

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:14.273
some of your scientific
background with you

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:17.343
when you started
writing fiction.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.814
-I think science is a lot like
a cult, in that

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.615
once you are indoctrinated,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:25.618
you never quite
get out of that mind-set.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.954
I think once you're trained
as a scientist,

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:30.356
you're a scientist forever.
And, yeah.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.659
And so there are many, many ways
in which my scientific training

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.596
influences my writing,
though I guess the most obvious

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.165
of which is that science
teaches you how to, like,

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:43.202
do a very strenuous long-term
project, the outcome of which

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:47.173
is very uncertain, and, like,
that is just writing, honestly.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:52.178
-You -- I've read that you enjoy
putting your work

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:55.514
in academic settings,
and one of the reasons is,

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.283
there was not a lot
of representation when you

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.619
read books
that took places there.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:03.088
I should say you never really
saw yourself in any of those.

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:05.925
-Yeah. I mean, I --
You know like many people,

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:09.028
I spent most of my life
on a campus.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.364
And I spent a lot of time
in academia,

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.265
and I love reading about
academia.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:14.734
There's just something about
those professors

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.370
getting up to hijinks that I
just cannot get enough of.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.605
But, yeah, you're right.
Like, time and again,

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:21.507
when I read those books,
I always felt like,

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:23.309
"Well, there are no Black people
in these books.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.011
There are no queer people
in these books."

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.947
And yet there are Black, queer
people on campus,

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:31.384
as I am one of them,
and so when I started writing

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:33.052
my first stories,
it just made sense

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.920
that I would write about
a setting that I was

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.089
deeply familiar with,
and I felt like I was getting

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:40.359
to correct the record
a little bit by writing myself

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:43.762
and people like me
into those stories.

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:47.600
-So, two of the characters
are dancers in the book.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.436
And it is written about
so beautifully

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.138
that I was wondering
if you yourself

00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:55.574
had a background in dance.

00:02:55.574 --> 00:02:58.378
-Only as, like, an intense
superfan and spectator.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.847
I always wanted
to be a dancer as a child,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:03.049
but my mom always
forbade me from it

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:04.617
because she thought
it would make my gay.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:06.719
But then, like,
I turned out to be gay anyway...

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.087
[ Laughter ]
...and so if it I could

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.957
go back in time, I would be
like, "Just let him dance.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.959
It's gonna be --
It's gonna be fine."

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:17.797
But, no, a lot of the dance
in the book just comes from

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.266
really loving dance
and being a dance enthusiast

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:23.602
and reading everything
I possibly could about it

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:26.005
to find out, you know,
like, "What are dancers like?"

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.474
And turns out
they're like the rest of us --

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:32.278
anxious, petty,
competitive, nervous.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:35.848
-Were there books --
You mention that you read a lot.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.316
You read
when you were very young.

00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:39.084
Is that true, as well?

00:03:39.084 --> 00:03:40.219
-Yeah.
Oh, yeah.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.221
I was a compulsive reader.

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:43.922
Reading everything
I could get my hands on.

00:03:43.922 --> 00:03:45.791
-And what was it
that you could get your hands on

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:47.593
when you were young?
What were the books that

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:49.228
influenced you,
or I should say,

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.631
were the first things
that influenced you?

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.533
-Yeah, so I --
The first books that I read

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:58.304
as, like, a young child
were romance novels.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.406
Because my aunt was
the only person in my family

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.507
who was really a big reader.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.142
And so I was reading
the books at hand,

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.244
and the books at hand
were these like, you know,

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:09.548
$2 bodice-rippers from the
grocery store and from Walmart.

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:13.286
But, to me, they were, you know,
the pinnacle of literature.

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.321
And to this day,
I still have a real

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:18.757
sort of fondness
for romance novels.

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:22.795
-You know, I didn't realize
exactly how intense the feedback

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:27.734
you get in writers workshops
is from your fellow writers.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:31.504
Do you find that helpful,
or, on an ego level,

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:34.774
is it just the most brutal thing
in the world?

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.643
-I think both.
You know, there's always a --

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:40.146
there's always a little nugget
of something helpful,

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:42.247
I think, in every
little bit of feedback.

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:44.683
But sometimes you've got
to really rummage around

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:47.286
inside of it
to find the part that's helpful.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.489
But it was,
I mean, incredibly brutal.

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:52.658
I mean, people talking about
your work for two hours,

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:55.661
taking it apart
on the molecular level almost,

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.497
and you weren't allowed
to talk back.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:05:00.466
So it was just like
getting kicked in the teeth

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.869
over and over again, you know,
in the place

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.137
where you're most sensitive --
your art.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:07.406
-Oh, so you're not allowed
to respond at all

00:05:07.406 --> 00:05:08.807
as you're getting
real-time criticism

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:10.309
from your fellow writers?

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:13.312
-So this is one thing that's,
like, changing in sort of

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.014
creative-writing programs
across the country,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:18.984
but when I did my MFA,
we were not allowed to respond.

00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:22.088
And, I mean, my first workshop
teacher even told us

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.723
not to even look at the person

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:25.024
as we were talking
about their work.

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.692
[ Laughter ]
Like it was very --

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:30.162
It was very hostile
and confrontational.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:34.033
-You also -- You have a robust
Twitter presence.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.535
Do you enjoy --
What is the thing you most enjoy

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:40.206
using Twitter for?
-Um...

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:44.076
Many -- Sowing chaos, of course,
like everyone else.

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:46.546
I think the thing that I love
most about Twitter is that you

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:51.584
can find a fandom or a community
based around almost anything.

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:56.155
Like, I've recently gotten very
into like niche pottery Twitter.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:58.957
That's a real fun place
to lurk around.

00:05:58.957 --> 00:06:01.727
And so I just think that Twitter
is full of these interesting

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.863
ready-made communities,

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:06.899
but sometimes it goes
real dark real fast.

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.701
-I want to give you credit.
because "Mare of Easttown,"

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:13.005
which was a show you and I
both were watching.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:17.677
You called it.
You called the twist weeks out.

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.679
-Yes.
I felt very smug about it.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:23.181
I felt very proud.
I will say that the day before

00:06:23.181 --> 00:06:25.952
the finale, I went to a barbecue
with some friends,

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:29.121
and we were swapping theories,
as one does,

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:30.923
and I shared my theory,
and all my friends were like,

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.358
"No, no,
I don't think that's it.

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:35.261
I don't think -- No, because
how would this have happened?"

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:36.929
And I was like,
"No, I think it's it."

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:41.100
And then, when it happened, I,
of course, texted all of them

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.901
very smugly...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.437
...to let them know
that I had, in fact,

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:48.474
seen it with my third eye,
my second sight. Yes.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:50.376
-You were like,
"I think the killer

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.112
is gonna look like a kid
from Los Angeles."

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:54.613
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.613 --> 00:06:58.317
Have you watched
"Tiny Secret Whispers" yourself?

00:06:58.317 --> 00:06:59.485
-Oh, yeah.
I'm a big fan.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:00.686
I'm a super fan.
I've got a group chat.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:02.221
-I mean, did you think --
All in all,

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.224
did you think it paid off?
-Yes.

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.792
I think that
there was a great arc.

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:08.994
I think the first couple
episodes, real slow.

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:11.830
But then the titration
of information at the end...

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.398
-Yeah.
-Exquisite.

00:07:13.398 --> 00:07:16.168
-I mean, I think you said it
better than anyone has.

00:07:16.168 --> 00:07:19.038
[ Laughter ]

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.340
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.941
Congratulations on your book.

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:25.378
It's wonderful.
-Thanks so much.

